1
00:00:01,180 --> 00:00:03,120
Did Apollo 9 almost fail?

2
00:00:03,240 --> 00:00:04,380
You may never have heard of it,

3
00:00:04,480 --> 00:00:07,900
but this ten‑day mission in March 1969 was

4
00:00:07,960 --> 00:00:10,220
the first flight of the full Apollo spacecraft

5
00:00:10,760 --> 00:00:11,840
— command module,

6
00:00:12,320 --> 00:00:12,780
service module,

7
00:00:13,120 --> 00:00:15,200
and lunar module flying together as one.

8
00:00:16,180 --> 00:00:19,380
A single engine misfire nearly derailed the entire Moon program.

9
00:00:20,280 --> 00:00:21,760
Here is what actually happened,

10
00:00:22,040 --> 00:00:22,920
from NASA's own records.

11
00:00:24,360 --> 00:00:28,520
Three men flew Apollo 9. Commander James A. McDivitt,

12
00:00:28,780 --> 00:00:32,100
a Gemini 4 veteran and commander of Apollo 9,

13
00:00:32,460 --> 00:00:34,260
first flight of the complete set of Apollo

14
00:00:34,260 --> 00:00:37,440
hardware and first flight of the Lunar Module.

15
00:00:38,040 --> 00:00:40,640
Command Module Pilot David R. Scott,

16
00:00:40,740 --> 00:00:43,090
who flew Gemini 8 and Apollo 9,

17
00:00:43,400 --> 00:00:45,480
later commanded Apollo 15,

18
00:00:45,480 --> 00:00:49,480
and logged 546 hours and 54 minutes in space

19
00:00:49,680 --> 00:00:53,480
including 20 hours 46 minutes of EVA; he later became

20
00:00:53,480 --> 00:00:56,640
Director of NASA's Dryden Flight Research Center.

21
00:00:57,240 --> 00:00:59,760
Lunar Module Pilot Russell L. Schweickart,

22
00:01:00,140 --> 00:01:03,090
from NASA's third astronaut group in October 1963,

23
00:01:03,840 --> 00:01:08,240
logged 241 hours in space on Apollo 9 — the first manned flight

24
00:01:08,240 --> 00:01:11,600
of the Lunar Module; during EVA tested the portable life support

25
00:01:11,740 --> 00:01:14,900
system backpack subsequently used on lunar surface explorations.

26
00:01:15,860 --> 00:01:16,460
Their backup crew:

27
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:19,380
Charles Conrad Jr. (CDR),

28
00:01:19,560 --> 00:01:21,020
Alan L. Bean (LMP),

29
00:01:21,720 --> 00:01:24,060
Richard F. Gordon Jr. (CMP).

30
00:01:25,220 --> 00:01:26,560
The two spacecraft had names:

31
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:28,180
the Lunar Module,

32
00:01:28,360 --> 00:01:30,050
nicknamed Spider (LM‑3),

33
00:01:30,420 --> 00:01:32,270
and the Command and Service Module,

34
00:01:32,580 --> 00:01:34,770
nicknamed Gumdrop (CSM‑104).

35
00:01:36,180 --> 00:01:39,240
This was the first manned flight of the LM — the first

36
00:01:39,240 --> 00:01:41,700
manned flight of all lunar hardware in Earth orbit.

37
00:01:42,920 --> 00:01:45,390
Two hours and 41 minutes after launch,

38
00:01:45,480 --> 00:01:47,380
CSM Gumdrop turned to face Spider,

39
00:01:47,820 --> 00:01:50,590
still attached to the S‑IVB third stage,

40
00:01:50,720 --> 00:01:54,040
and completed the first successful docking of the Apollo program.

41
00:01:54,700 --> 00:01:55,630
About an hour later,

42
00:01:56,020 --> 00:01:59,780
springs ejected the docked spacecraft from the S‑IVB. The

43
00:01:59,780 --> 00:02:02,850
crew pressurized the tunnel between Gumdrop and Spider,

44
00:02:02,980 --> 00:02:04,740
removed the CM hatch,

45
00:02:04,900 --> 00:02:07,360
connected umbilicals to power the LM while docked,

46
00:02:07,840 --> 00:02:09,640
then replaced the hatch.

47
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:11,240
On flight day 3,

48
00:02:11,540 --> 00:02:14,860
McDivitt and Schweickart put on spacesuits and transferred to the

49
00:02:14,860 --> 00:02:18,540
LM through the tunnel connection to perform a systems checkout.

50
00:02:18,860 --> 00:02:23,200
This included a 367‑second firing of the LM descent engine

51
00:02:23,520 --> 00:02:26,340
to simulate the throttle pattern to be used during a lunar

52
00:02:26,340 --> 00:02:30,560
landing mission — McDivitt controlled the final 59 seconds,

53
00:02:30,980 --> 00:02:34,200
varying the thrust from 10 to 40 percent and shutting it off

54
00:02:34,200 --> 00:02:37,600
manually — the first crewed throttling of an engine in space.

55
00:02:38,540 --> 00:02:41,420
They also tested the landing and rendezvous radars and

56
00:02:41,420 --> 00:02:44,660
made a 5‑minute television broadcast from inside Spider.

57
00:02:45,600 --> 00:02:48,380
The flight plan called for a two‑hour EVA to simulate

58
00:02:48,630 --> 00:02:51,340
external transfer rescue techniques — the only

59
00:02:51,340 --> 00:02:54,420
planned EVA from the LM before an actual lunar landing.

60
00:02:55,340 --> 00:02:58,000
But Schweickart suffered space motion sickness,

61
00:02:58,340 --> 00:03:00,020
vomiting twice during the first two days,

62
00:03:00,440 --> 00:03:02,700
so the EVA was delayed and then shortened

63
00:03:02,700 --> 00:03:06,370
from a planned 2 hours to 37.5 minutes.

64
00:03:06,600 --> 00:03:07,160
Instead,

65
00:03:07,320 --> 00:03:11,360
he climbed out of the LM porch for a 37.5‑minute EVA,

66
00:03:11,740 --> 00:03:14,060
testing the EVA mobility unit including

67
00:03:14,200 --> 00:03:16,260
the portable life support system backpack

68
00:03:16,880 --> 00:03:20,020
— this was the first and only EVA before the lunar landing

69
00:03:20,360 --> 00:03:23,540
and the only opportunity to test the PLSS in space.

70
00:03:24,780 --> 00:03:27,380
Schweickart floated out the LM side hatch onto the front porch

71
00:03:27,380 --> 00:03:31,400
using gold‑painted foot restraints ("golden slippers"); Scott

72
00:03:31,400 --> 00:03:34,640
opened the CM side hatch and floated partway out (stand‑up

73
00:03:35,280 --> 00:03:37,790
EVA); both spent about 37 minutes outside.

74
00:03:38,460 --> 00:03:42,520
Schweickart's only connection to the LM was a 25‑foot nylon rope;

75
00:03:42,520 --> 00:03:45,380
he maneuvered on handrails near the hatch (found them easy),

76
00:03:45,940 --> 00:03:46,480
took photographs,

77
00:03:46,960 --> 00:03:50,980
and described rain squalls over KSC. Scott performed a stand‑up

78
00:03:50,980 --> 00:03:55,470
EVA (about 1 hour 4 minutes) from the CM hatch without a PLSS,

79
00:03:56,180 --> 00:03:57,980
connected to CM life support via

80
00:03:57,980 --> 00:03:59,840
umbilicals and a Pressure Control Valve,

81
00:04:00,200 --> 00:04:01,220
and filmed Schweickart.

82
00:04:02,420 --> 00:04:04,460
Scott stood in the open side hatch of Gumdrop

83
00:04:04,580 --> 00:04:07,440
observing Schweickart's EVA activities.

84
00:04:07,580 --> 00:04:10,520
Mission Control called it a complete success — the first

85
00:04:10,620 --> 00:04:14,980
and only spacesuit/PLSS test before the Moon landing.

86
00:04:15,560 --> 00:04:19,360
Flight day 5: McDivitt and Schweickart undocked in Spider for a 6

87
00:04:19,360 --> 00:04:22,900
hour 23‑minute independent flight while Scott remained in Gumdrop.

88
00:04:23,840 --> 00:04:25,950
About 45 minutes after undocking,

89
00:04:26,300 --> 00:04:31,400
Spider's DPS engine fired 19 seconds (first at 10% thrust then throttled

90
00:04:32,060 --> 00:04:36,060
to 40%) beginning separation and placing it about 50 miles from

91
00:04:36,250 --> 00:04:41,400
Gumdrop; a 22‑second DPS burn opened the distance to about 100 miles.

92
00:04:42,360 --> 00:04:45,500
To begin rendezvous Spider fired ascent‑stage RCS

93
00:04:45,520 --> 00:04:48,980
thrusters for 32 seconds while jettisoning the Descent

94
00:04:49,040 --> 00:04:52,520
Stage (which remained in orbit until March 22,

95
00:04:53,160 --> 00:04:58,040
burning up over the Indian Ocean); first APS burn lasted 3 seconds,

96
00:04:58,620 --> 00:05:01,200
second APS burn 38 seconds put Spider

97
00:05:01,200 --> 00:05:02,840
on an intercept course with Gumdrop;

98
00:05:03,760 --> 00:05:06,140
two small course corrections refined the trajectory.

99
00:05:07,600 --> 00:05:10,180
Spider stopped about 100 feet from Gumdrop for

100
00:05:10,180 --> 00:05:12,820
a pitchover maneuver; Scott photographed the

101
00:05:12,840 --> 00:05:16,000
ascent stage including its engine ("the biggest,

102
00:05:16,200 --> 00:05:16,440
friendliest,

103
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:19,410
funniest looking Spider I've ever seen");

104
00:05:19,720 --> 00:05:21,420
McDivitt brought Spider in for smooth

105
00:05:22,360 --> 00:05:25,260
docking; the craft flew separately for 6 hours 23 minutes.

106
00:05:26,480 --> 00:05:28,980
Two hours after docking McDivitt and Schweickart

107
00:05:29,100 --> 00:05:32,100
rejoined Scott in Gumdrop and shortly after

108
00:05:32,100 --> 00:05:35,820
jettisoned Spider; Mission Control commanded Spider's

109
00:05:36,360 --> 00:05:39,360
APS to fire for six minutes into a highly elliptical

110
00:05:39,380 --> 00:05:42,640
Earth orbit from which it did not decay until October

111
00:05:42,640 --> 00:05:48,100
23 1981. Apollo 9 was the first manned flight of the

112
00:05:48,980 --> 00:05:52,080
LM; first manned flight of all lunar hardware in Earth orbit.

113
00:05:52,920 --> 00:05:56,500
The mission demonstrated docked and undocked maneuvers,

114
00:05:56,620 --> 00:05:58,840
performed a lunar module–command module rendezvous,

115
00:05:59,160 --> 00:06:01,760
and demonstrated extra‑vehicular capability —

116
00:06:02,340 --> 00:06:05,660
everything Apollo 11 would need to land on the Moon.

117
00:06:05,720 --> 00:06:08,300
The backpack Schweickart tested in space was

118
00:06:08,300 --> 00:06:11,390
subsequently used on the lunar surface explorations.

119
00:06:12,220 --> 00:06:13,600
Every system that would later carry

120
00:06:13,620 --> 00:06:15,760
humans to the Moon had been flown,

121
00:06:16,180 --> 00:06:16,740
tested,

122
00:06:16,740 --> 00:06:18,000
and proven in Earth orbit.

123
00:06:19,300 --> 00:06:21,400
Every claim in this video comes from NASA primary

124
00:06:21,710 --> 00:06:25,590
sources: the NASA Apollo 9 Mission Details page,

125
00:06:25,840 --> 00:06:26,990
NASA mission pages,

126
00:06:27,320 --> 00:06:28,760
NASA history articles,

127
00:06:28,880 --> 00:06:32,620
the Lunar and Planetary Institute Apollo 9 mission overview,

128
00:06:32,700 --> 00:06:33,780
the Apollo Flight Journal,

129
00:06:34,140 --> 00:06:36,900
and the Apollo 9 Mission Report (MSC‑PA‑R‑69‑2,

130
00:06:39,200 --> 00:06:45,180
May 1969). All are U.S. government works in the public domain; NASA

131
00:06:45,180 --> 00:06:52,160
logos and insignia are restricted by law (14 CFR 1221). If you see

132
00:06:52,180 --> 00:06:55,840
a claim about Apollo 9 that doesn't trace back to a primary source,

133
00:06:56,220 --> 00:06:57,060
ask to see the document.

134
00:06:58,180 --> 00:06:59,620
That's how you know what really happened.

